    "engine": "julia",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Julia essentials for modelers\"\ncourse-unit: p01-julia-essentials\nstatus: complete\n---\n\n## Why Julia\n\n- Scientific computing is usually split across **two languages** — a convenient\n  one to write the analysis, a fast one for the parts that are too slow.\n- Julia is interactive and dynamically typed like R, but **compiles to native\n  code**: the model kernel you write is the code that runs inside the solver.\n- Epidemic models are run thousands of times over — ensembles, sensitivity\n  sweeps, inference — so this is not an abstract concern.\n\n## Why this unit matters\n\n- The course reuses one object throughout: the SIR model of Kermack & McKendrick (1927).\n- Before solving it we need a precise slice of Julia:\n  - the **REPL** and its modes;\n  - **in-place kernels** `f(du, u, p, t)` — the SciML calling convention;\n  - **broadcasting** and comprehensions to evaluate models on a grid;\n  - **named tuples** for parameters, read by field, and **multiple dispatch**;\n  - **reproducible randomness** with `StableRNGs`.\n- Goal: the vocabulary, not a language tour.\n\n## The REPL has four modes {.smaller}\n\nType one character at an empty prompt; `Backspace` returns.\n\n| Key | Mode | For |\n|---|---|---|\n| — | Julia | evaluating expressions (default) |\n| `?` | help | `?expm1` — docstring, signatures, examples |\n| `]` | package | `]st`, `]instantiate` — and it names the active environment |\n| `;` | shell | `ls`, `cd`, `git status` without leaving Julia |\n\n- The package prompt names the **active environment** — check it first when a\n  package seems missing. Start Julia with `--project=.` from the repo root.\n- Greek letters are ordinary names: `\\beta`⇥ → `β`, `\\gamma`⇥ → `γ`,\n  `\\lambda`⇥ → `λ`. ASCII works identically.\n\n## The running model {.smaller}\n\nDeterministic, continuous-time, population SIR with force of infection\n$\\lambda = \\beta c I/N$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}S}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\lambda S,\\quad\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\lambda S - \\gamma I,\\quad\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}R}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\gamma I.\n$$ {#eq-s-p01-sir}\n\nThe three right-hand sides sum to zero, so $S+I+R=N$ is conserved.\nRate → per-step probability for later discretisation:\n$p(r,\\delta t)=1-e^{-r\\,\\delta t}$.\n\n## Julia API: an in-place kernel\n\n`u = [S, I, R]` maps onto @eq-s-p01-sir; `p` carries $(\\beta,c,\\gamma,N)$.\n\n::: {#cell-s-p01-kernel .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nfunction sir_ode!(du, u, p, t)\n    S, I, R = u                    # state: positional\n    λ = p.β * p.c * I / p.N        # parameters: by field\n    du[1] = -λ * S                # dS/dt\n    du[2] = λ * S - p.γ * I       # dI/dt\n    du[3] = p.γ * I               # dR/dt\n    return nothing\nend\n\nu0 = [990.0, 10.0, 0.0]\np  = (β = 0.05, c = 10.0, γ = 0.25, N = 1000.0)\ndu = zeros(3); sir_ode!(du, u0, p, 0.0); du\n```\n\n::: {#s-p01-kernel .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n -4.95\n  2.45\n  2.5\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nState is read by position, parameters by name — `p.β`, never `p[1]`.\n\n## Broadcasting & a figure from code\n\n::: {#cell-fig-s-p01-rate .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nusing CairoMakie\nrate_to_proportion(r, δt) = -expm1(-r * δt)   # stable 1 - exp(-r δt)\nδts = 0.0:0.05:2.0\nfig = Figure(size = (760, 360))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = \"δt\", ylabel = \"event probability\")\nlines!(ax, δts, rate_to_proportion.(1.0, δts);  linewidth = 3, label = \"r = 1.0\")\nlines!(ax, δts, rate_to_proportion.(0.25, δts); linewidth = 3, label = \"r = 0.25\")\naxislegend(ax; position = :rb); fig\n```\n\n::: {#fig-s-p01-rate .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<img width=760 height=360 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n```\n\nRate-to-proportion $1-e^{-r\\delta t}$ for a fast ($r=1$) and slow ($r=0.25$) rate: broadcasting evaluates the curve with no explicit loop.\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Dispatch & reproducible randomness\n\n::: {#cell-s-p01-dispatch .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nusing StableRNGs\nfoi(I::Number, p)         = p.β * p.c * I / p.N\nfoi(u::AbstractVector, p) = foi(u[2], p)      # read I from the state\n\nrng = StableRNG(2024)               # local, version-stable stream\n(foi(10.0, p), foi(u0, p), rand(rng, 3))\n```\n\n::: {#s-p01-dispatch .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(0.005, 0.005, [0.8177021060277301, 0.3804323567786363, 0.5425813266814807])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nOne name, two methods, no `if` inspecting the arguments. You will rarely *write*\ndispatch here — but it is why `solve(prob, Tsit5())` needs no glue: the method is\nchosen by the types of both arguments, so swapping in a stiff solver, a\nsensitivity package, or an inference package leaves your kernel untouched.\n\n## Validation\n\nThe derivatives must sum to zero (conservation) and `rate_to_proportion`\nmust stay a valid probability and match its Taylor limit $p\\approx r\\,\\delta t$:\n\n::: {#cell-s-p01-check .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nsir_ode!(du, u0, p, 0.0)\nconserved = isapprox(sum(du), 0.0; atol = 1e-12)\ntaylor    = isapprox(rate_to_proportion(0.25, 1e-6), 0.25e-6; rtol = 1e-3)\n(conserved, taylor)\n```\n\n::: {#s-p01-check .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(true, true)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nBoth `true`: the kernel conserves population and the conversion is well behaved.\n\n## Limitations & takeaways\n\n- **Scope:** this is not a full Julia course — no package management beyond\n  `using`, no profiling, metaprogramming, user-defined `struct`s, or parametric\n  types.\n- **Takeaways:**\n  - write mutating kernels with the `f(du, u, p, t)` signature;\n  - broadcast with `.` and fuse operations; use `expm1` for stability;\n  - state by position (`S, I, R = u`), parameters by field (`p.β`);\n  - dispatch is why `solve` composes with any problem/algorithm pair;\n  - seed a *local* `StableRNG` for bit-reproducible figures.\n- Next: we actually *solve* the model in **Foundations**.\n\n::: {.notes}\nDemonstrate the four REPL modes live rather than reading the table — enter and\nleave each one, and show `?expm1` in particular, since help mode answers most\n\"what arguments does this take\" questions faster than a web search and works\noffline. Point out the environment name in the package prompt; a package that\nappears to be missing is almost always Julia started outside the project.\nEmphasise that the `!` suffix is a convention, not syntax, and that in-place\nkernels avoid per-evaluation allocations across the thousands of solver calls.\nSpend a moment on the two reading conventions — state destructured positionally,\nparameters read by field — because every chapter from here follows it, and a\nreordered positional parameter tuple is the failure that still runs and returns\nwrong numbers. On dispatch, resist the urge to teach the type system: the point\nparticipants need is that `solve(prob, Tsit5())` works without glue code, which\nis what makes the solver swap in later chapters a one-word change.\nStress that `StableRNG` output is fixed across Julia versions, unlike the global\ndefault RNG, which is why every stochastic figure in the course is reproducible.\nPoint listeners to the book for the full narrative and to the Pluto notebook to\nvary the baseline interactively.\n:::\n\n## Learn more\n\n- Book chapter: [Julia essentials for modelers](../../prerequisites/01-julia-essentials.html)\n- Pluto notebook: [`01-julia-essentials.jl`](../../notebooks/prerequisites/01-julia-essentials.jl)\n- [Julia manual](https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/) ·\n  [CairoMakie](https://docs.makie.org/stable/)\n\n",
